<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  driving.xml — what the accessibility service is allowed to do.

  History
    2026-08-09  A. Sigdel  Created.

  canRetrieveWindowContent is the one that fails silently. Without it the
  service binds, appears enabled, and answers null from rootInActiveWindow for
  the life of the install, with nothing in the log to point at.

  The event types are declared because a service with none is not listed in
  Settings at all. Nothing consumes them: DrivingService reads the tree when it
  needs it, for the reason its header gives.

  No canPerformGestures, and none is coming. Every action turned out to be a
  node action — click, set text, scroll forward — so no coordinate enters the
  app at all, which is stronger than how-the-agent-drives.md promised.

  flagRequestAccessibilityButton is the summon. It puts a control in the
  navigation bar, or a floating one where there is no bar, and it is the
  service's own — the assistant role was declined in the design review because
  AssistStructure is read-only and would displace the person's real assistant.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged|typeWindowContentChanged"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault|flagRequestAccessibilityButton"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:description="@string/driving_description"
    android:notificationTimeout="100" />
